  "query_preview": "..\n(Saturday night through Thursday)\nIssued at 525 AM EDT Fri Jul 24 2026\n\nThe overall pattern as we move into the new workweek will see a \nbroad and deep high pressure system remain parked over the southern \nPlains and a deep layer trough dominate the Mid-Atlantic and \nNortheastern states.  This pattern will remain in place through the \nend of the week.  This will result in a sustained northerly flow \npattern across the forecast area.  As this northerly flow regime \ntakes hold, drier air will advect into the mid and upper levels, and \nthis will help push PWATS down to around 1.6 to 1.7 inches from \nMonday through Wednesday.  These values are near average for this \ntime of year, and indicate that enough moisture will remain in place \nin the low levels to support some isolated convective development \neach afternoon.  The threat of isolated convective development, \ngenerally once the convective temperature in the mid 90s is reached \nin the late afternoon and early evening hours, is further supported \nby mid-level lapse rates in excess of 6.5 C/KM and MLCAPE in excess \nof 1500 J/KG each afternoon. One of the bigger concerns with these \nisolated convective cells is the potential for strong to \noccasionally severe thunderstorm wind gusts occurring as the cells \nentrain the dry mid-level air into their updrafts.  Evaporative \ncooling within the updraft could occur, and this could support a few \nmicroburst events each day as the cooler and heavier air exceeds the \nupdraft strength resulting in thunderstorm collapse.  DCAPE values \nof 1100 to 1300 J/KG support this concern.  \n\nBefore the convection develops, temperatures will climb into the mid \n90s south of the fall line and into the upper 80s and lower 90s \nnorth of the fall line in the North Georgia mountains.  Conditions \nwill remain very humid in the low levels, so heat index values will \neasily climb to 100 to 103 on Monday and then rise further to 103 to \n108 on Tuesday and Wednesday from I-85 southward, including metro \nAtlanta.  Heat advisories will likely be needed as we move into \nTuesday and Wednesday.  \n\nAll of the medium range guidance is in good agreement that \nreinforcing and much stronger northern stream trough will dig into \nthe eastern seaboard on Thursday and Friday.  This will drive a very \nstrong front for late July through the region Wednesday evening into \nWednesday night.  Behind the front, a very dry airmass as noted by \nPWATS falling to around an inch, or near the 10th percentile for \nthis time of year, will move into the area.  This will effectively \nkill off any rain chances due to a lack of moisture and instability. \nAlthough temperatures will remain near average, heat index values \nwill fall back below advisory levels and range closer to 100 degrees \non Thursday and Friday.  Overnight lows may also feel almost \npleasant as readings dip into the 60s in the mountains and lower 70s \nfrom Atlanta southward.",
